DevOps & Git – Practical Exam Guide
All 10 Experiments – Step by Step


**Experiment 1: Initialize a Git Repository**
Goal: Create a local Git repository, add a file, and commit using Git commands.
Prerequisites
•
•
Install Git from https://git-scm.com
Open Terminal (Linux/Mac) or Git Bash (Windows)
Step-by-Step Commands
Step 1 – Create a project folder:
mkdir myproject
cd myproject
Step 2 – Initialize the Git repository:
git init
📌 Note: This creates a hidden .git folder that tracks all changes.
Step 3 – Create a text file:
echo "Hello, Git!" > hello.txt
Step 4 – Check the status:
git status
📌 Note: You will see hello.txt listed as an untracked file.
Step 5 – Stage the file (add to index):
git add hello.txt
Or to add all files at once:
git add .
Step 6 – Commit the file:
git commit -m "Initial commit: added hello.txt"
Step 7 – View commit history:
git log
Expected Output
•
•
•
git init → Initialized empty Git repository
git status → shows hello.txt as new file
git log → shows your first commit with message


**Experiment 2: Push Local Project to GitHub**
Goal: Create a GitHub repository and push your local project to it.
Prerequisites
•
•
GitHub account created at github.com
Git installed and configured with your name and email
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – Configure Git with your identity (do this once):
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
Step 2 – Create a new repository on GitHub:
•
Go to github.com → click + → New repository
•
Give it a name e.g. myproject
•
Do NOT check 'Initialize this repository' (we already have local files)
•
Click Create repository
Step 3 – Link local repo to GitHub (copy the URL from GitHub):
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/myproject.git
Step 4 – Rename the default branch to main:
git branch -M main
Step 5 – Push your code to GitHub:
git push -u origin main
📌 Note: -u sets upstream so future pushes just need: git push
Step 6 – Verify on GitHub by refreshing the page. Your file should appear.
Key Commands Summary
•
git remote add origin <URL> – connects local to remote
•
git push -u origin main – pushes and sets tracking


**Experiment 3: Branches and Merging in Git**
Goal: Create two branches, modify a file in each, then merge into main.
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – Start from main branch:
git checkout main
Step 2 – Create and switch to branch1:
git checkout -b branch1
📌 Note: git checkout -b creates a new branch AND switches to it.
Step 3 – Modify the file in branch1:
echo "Change from branch1" >> hello.txt
git add hello.txt
git commit -m "Changes from branch1"
Step 4 – Switch back to main and create branch2:
git checkout main
git checkout -b branch2
Step 5 – Modify the file in branch2:
echo "Change from branch2" >> hello.txt
git add hello.txt
git commit -m "Changes from branch2"
Step 6 – Merge branch1 into main:
git checkout main
git merge branch1
Step 7 – Merge branch2 into main:
git merge branch2
Step 8 – View final file:
cat hello.txt
Key Concepts
•
•
Branch – an independent line of development
Merge – combining changes from two branches into one
•
HEAD – points to the currently active branch


**Experiment 4: Pull Request and Merge Conflict**
Goal: Create a pull request on GitHub and resolve a merge conflict.
Part A – Create a Pull Request
Step 1 – Create a new branch and push it:
git checkout -b feature-branch
echo "Feature work" >> hello.txt
git add . && git commit -m 'Feature update'
git push origin feature-branch
Step 2 – On GitHub:
•
Go to your repo → you will see 'Compare & pull request' button
•
Click it → add a title and description
•
Click Create pull request
•
Click Merge pull request → Confirm merge
Part B – Create and Resolve a Merge Conflict
Step 1 – On main, edit the file:
git checkout main
echo "Main branch line" > conflict.txt
git add . && git commit -m 'Main edit'
Step 2 – On a new branch, edit the same file differently:
git checkout -b conflict-branch
echo "Conflict branch line" > conflict.txt
git add . && git commit -m 'Conflict branch edit'
Step 3 – Switch to main and merge (conflict will occur):
git checkout main
git merge conflict-branch
Step 4 – Open conflict.txt, you will see:
<<<<<<< HEAD
Main branch line
=======
Conflict branch line
>>>>>>> conflict-branch
Step 5 – Manually edit to keep what you want, then:
git add conflict.txt
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflict"
📌 Note: Always remove the <<<, ===, >>> markers when resolving conflicts.


**Experiment 5: GitHub Actions – Build Success Workflow**
Goal: Configure a GitHub Actions workflow that prints a success message on every push.
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – In your repo, create the workflow directory:
mkdir -p .github/workflows
Step 2 – Create the workflow file:
nano .github/workflows/hello.yml
Step 3 – Paste this content into the file:
name: Hello World Workflow
on:
push:
branches: [ main ]
jobs:
greet:
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
steps:
- name: Checkout code
uses: actions/checkout@v3
- name: Print Success Message
run: echo 'Build Successful! Workflow triggered on push.
'
Step 4 – Save, commit, and push:
git add .github/workflows/hello.yml
git commit -m "Add GitHub Actions workflow"
git push origin main
Step 5 – On GitHub, go to Actions tab to see the workflow running.
Key YAML Terms
•
•
•
•
on: push – trigger when code is pushed
runs-on – the OS of the runner machine (ubuntu-latest)
steps – individual tasks inside a job
run – shell command to execute


**Experiment 6: CI/CD Workflow – Build and Deploy HTML Page**
Goal: Use GitHub Actions to build and deploy a static HTML page automatically.
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – Create a simple HTML file in your repo:
nano index.html
Paste this:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
</html>
<body><h1>Deployed via GitHub Actions!</h1></body>
Step 2 – Create the CI/CD workflow file:
nano .github/workflows/deploy.yml
Paste this:
name: Deploy Static Page
on:
push:
branches: [ main ]
jobs:
deploy:
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
steps:
- uses: actions/checkout@v3
- name: Deploy to GitHub Pages
uses: peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3
with:
github
_
publish
token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB
dir: ./
TOKEN }}
_
_
Step 3 – Enable GitHub Pages:
•
•
•
Go to repo → Settings → Pages
Under Source, select gh-pages branch
Click Save
Step 4 – Push the files:
git add .
git commit -m "Add CI/CD deploy workflow"
git push origin main
Step 5 – After the workflow runs, visit:
https://yourusername.github.io/myproject/
📌 Note: GITHUB_TOKEN is automatically provided by GitHub – no manual setup needed.


