Experiment 8: Dockerfile – Build a Docker Image
Goal: Create a Dockerfile for a simple web application and build a Docker image.
Prerequisites
•
Docker installed: https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/
•
Verify: docker --version
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – Create your app file (app.py):
from flask import Flask
app = Flask(
name
__
)
__
@app.route('/')
def home():
return 'Hello from Docker!'
if
name
== '
main
':
__
__
__
__
app.run(host='0.0.0.0'
, port=5000)
Step 2 – Create requirements.txt:
flask
Step 3 – Create the Dockerfile (no extension):
FROM python:3.9-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt
COPY . .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python"
,
"app.py"]
Step 4 – Build the Docker image:
docker build -t myflaskapp .
📌 Note: The dot (.) means 'use the Dockerfile in the current directory'.
Step 5 – Run the container:
docker run -p 5000:5000 myflaskapp
Step 6 – Open browser and visit: http://localhost:5000
Key Dockerfile Instructions
•
FROM – base image to start from
•
WORKDIR – sets working directory inside container
•
COPY – copies files from host to container
•
RUN – executes commands during build
•
EXPOSE – documents which port the app uses
•
CMD – default command to run when container starts
Experiment 9: Docker Compose – Web Server + Database
Goal: Use Docker Compose to run two containers: a web server (Nginx) and a database (MySQL).
Prerequisites
•
Docker and Docker Compose installed
•
Verify: docker compose version
Step-by-Step
Step 1 – Create a project folder:
mkdir compose-demo && cd compose-demo
Step 2 – Create docker-compose.yml:
version: '3.8'
services:
web:
image: nginx:latest
ports:
-
'8080:80'
depends
on:
_
- db
db:
image: mysql:5.7
environment:
MYSQL
ROOT
PASSWORD: rootpassword
_
_
MYSQL
DATABASE: mydb
_
ports:
-
'3306:3306'
Step 3 – Start both containers:
docker compose up -d
📌 Note: -d runs containers in the background (detached mode).
Step 4 – Verify containers are running:
docker compose ps
Step 5 – Visit the Nginx web server:
http://localhost:8080
Step 6 – Stop all containers:
docker compose down
Key Docker Compose Concepts
•
services – defines each container
•
•
•
•
image – which Docker image to use
ports – HOST:CONTAINER port mapping
environment – environment variables passed into the container
depends
on – ensures db starts before web
_
Experiment 10: Ansible Playbook – Install Apache/Nginx
Goal: Write and execute an Ansible playbook to install Apache or Nginx on a Linux machine.
Prerequisites
•
•
Ansible installed on control machine: sudo apt install ansible -y
Target Linux machine (can be localhost for practice)
Step-by-Step – Install Nginx
Step 1 – Check Ansible is installed:
ansible --version
Step 2 – Set up inventory file (hosts.ini):
[webservers]
localhost ansible
connection=local
_
📌 Note: Using ansible_connection=local means it runs on your own machine, no SSH needed.
Step 3 – Create the playbook file (install
_
nginx.yml):
---
- name: Install and Start Nginx
hosts: webservers
become: yes
tasks:
- name: Update apt cache
apt:
update
cache: yes
_
- name: Install Nginx
apt:
name: nginx
state: present
- name: Start Nginx service
service:
name: nginx
state: started
enabled: yes
Step 4 – Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i hosts.ini install
_
nginx.yml
Step 5 – Verify Nginx is running:
systemctl status nginx
Step 6 – Open browser and visit: http://localhost
For Apache Instead of Nginx
Simply replace nginx with apache2 in the playbook:
name: apache2
Key Ansible Terms
•
Playbook – a YAML file describing automation tasks
•
hosts – which machines to run tasks on
•
•
•
•
become: yes – run as sudo/root
tasks – list of actions to perform
apt module – manages packages on Ubuntu/Debian
service module – starts/stops/enables services
